  "report_text": "# FSMA 204 action brief for Lee’s Market\n**To:** Emily Patel, Regulatory Affairs Manager  \n**Case reference date:** 27 August 2025  \n**Regulatory status note checked:** 20 November 2026\n\n## Recommendation\nUse FSMA 204 as a lot-level operating discipline across covered suppliers, distribution, stores, fresh-food preparation, and e-commerce. Start with a product-and-facility scope map, capture lot data at receiving, retain lot links through any covered transformation or business-to-business shipment, and prove retrieval through mock trace exercises. That capability can support faster, narrower holds and more accurate customer notices. Lee’s should communicate only what its lot records verify; the internal records do not establish a food-safety-specific customer-trust gain or a completed traceability test. [1–4, 7–9]\n\n## What likely applies\nThe rule applies to foods on FDA’s Food Traceability List (FTL) and the critical tracking events (CTEs) a covered person performs, subject to exemptions. The likely Lee’s assortment to screen first includes leafy greens, fresh herbs, cucumbers, peppers, tomatoes, melons, tropical tree fruits, sprouts, fresh-cut produce, finfish, crustaceans, molluscan shellfish, shell eggs, non-hard cheeses, nut butters, and ready-to-eat deli salads. A product name alone is not enough: confirm ingredients, food state, processing, and regulatory jurisdiction for each SKU. Meat, bakery, and prepared-food offerings are not automatically covered; an FTL ingredient or a transformation can change the analysis. [1–3, 5]\n\nFor covered receiving events, configure capture of the product description, quantity and unit, receipt date and location, traceability lot code (TLC), TLC source, immediate previous source, and reference-document type/number. Covered entities retain records for two years and must provide them to FDA within 24 hours or another reasonable time agreed with FDA; FDA can also request an electronic sortable spreadsheet. Use existing ERP/WMS/POS tools if they reliably link and retrieve the required records; the rule does not require a particular commercial platform. [1, 3, 4]\n\nLee’s U.S. and Canadian footprint makes a facility and destination map important: confirm which legal entities and facilities handle FTL foods for U.S. consumption, what activities they perform, and which exemptions actually apply. Do not infer a retailer exemption from chain-wide revenue. Counsel should resolve borderline SKU, ingredient, transformation, and facility questions before the scope is approved. [1–3, 5]\n\n## Lee’s readiness and priorities\nInternal records say the inventory system tracked approximately 250 high-risk food products, nearly 30% of inventory, as of Q3 2024, while additional controls and data capture were still being implemented. A 15 July 2025 email reports an 85% compliance rate among high-risk food suppliers, but the underlying report, denominator, and definition were not retrieved. Treat both as leads for validation, not proof of FSMA 204 readiness. [7, 8]\n\nA 2023 simulated recall identified communication-protocol and supply-chain responsiveness issues; the company said it strengthened notification procedures and planned regular crisis-team training. The records do not provide a measured trace time or a later retest. An April 2024 training email reported 90% completion and an average of 12 training hours, but explicitly said that training was not FSMA 204-specific. [7, 9]\n\n## 30/60/90-day implementation\n**Days 0–30 — Scope and baseline.** Create one controlled matrix by SKU, ingredient/form, supplier, facility, destination, and CTE. Classify FTL items and flag store/DC receiving, store transfers, direct-to-consumer orders, and activities that may be transformation (such as cutting, repacking, seafood or deli preparation). Record legal decisions and exemptions with counsel. Baseline supplier data completeness, store receiving practices, and present traceback time. [1–3, 5]\n\n**Days 31–60 — Supplier and store workflows.** Issue a common supplier data specification for TLC, TLC source, previous source, product/quantity, and reference documents. Require these fields in shipment data or an agreed receiving document; route missing or conflicting lot data for resolution. At receiving, scan or record the TLC and source against the delivery and receiving location, then preserve the link to the inventory lot. Map DC-to-store and other business-to-business shipments separately. [1, 3, 4]\n\nFor online picking and delivery, retain an internal connection between inventory lots and affected orders so Lee’s can identify the relevant customers during a recall. FDA provides a limited shipping-record exception for certain direct-to-consumer shipments by retail food establishments; do not extend that exception to receiving, transformations, or business-to-business transfers without checking the facts. Preserve the customer-order link as an operational recall control even where a shipping KDE exception applies. [1, 3]\n\n**Days 61–90 — Pilot and prove.** Pilot at a distribution center and representative stores covering produce, seafood, and prepared-food workflows, including online orders. Run a backward trace from a customer order or store lot to supplier records and a forward trace from a supplier lot to all affected stores and orders. Measure missing KDEs, reconciliation errors, time to identify affected inventory/customers, and whether records can be assembled within FDA’s 24-hour access window. Train receivers, produce/seafood/deli teams, buyers, and recall staff on their specific steps; fix gaps before wider rollout. [1, 3, 7, 9]\n\n## Measures and customer communication\nTrack: (1) share of confirmed FTL SKUs mapped to facilities and events; (2) share of covered receipts complete for required KDEs; (3) supplier lot-data completeness and exception closure; (4) trace exercise time and affected-lot/order accuracy; (5) employee training completion by job; and (6) customer notice accuracy and timeliness. Set tighter internal trace targets after the pilot establishes a baseline. Add a food-safety confidence question to customer research rather than using general CRM engagement as a proxy. [1, 3, 7–9]\n\nWhen a trace identifies an issue, use verified lot and order data to hold only the implicated stock where supported, notify the right locations and customers, and give clear instructions. Do not market FSMA 204 compliance as a guarantee that food is risk-free. The rule’s traceability purpose supports faster identification and removal of potentially contaminated food, while Lee’s internal records report no food-safety-specific trust measure. [1, 7]\n\n## Timing\nAs of the case date, the original compliance date was 20 January 2026. FDA had announced an intention to extend it in March 2025 and, on 6 August 2025, proposed a 30-month extension to 20 July 2028; on 27 August 2025 that was a proposal, not a finalized amendment. FDA’s rule page checked on 20 November 2026 still describes the extension as proposed and reports a subsequent Congressional direction that FDA not enforce before 20 July 2028. Maintain the implementation plan and have counsel verify the operative date and enforcement position before relying on any delay. [1, 5, 6]\n\n## References\n[1] FDA, “FSMA Final Rule on Requirements for Additional Traceability Records” — https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-requirements-additional-traceability-records-certain-foods  \n[2] FDA, “Food Traceability List” — https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/food-traceability-list  \n[3] FDA, “Frequently Asked Questions: FSMA Food Traceability Rule” — https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/frequently-asked-questions-fsma-food-traceability-rule  \n[4] 21 CFR § 1.1345 — https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-1/subpart-S/subject-group-ECFRbfe98fb65ccc9f7/section-1.1345  \n[5] FDA, 6 Aug 2025, “FDA Proposes to Extend Compliance Date for Food Traceability Rule and Issues New FAQs and Other Resources” — https://www.fda.gov/food/hfp-constituent-updates/fda-proposes-extend-compliance-date-food-traceability-rule-and-issues-new-faqs-and-other-resources  \n[6] FDA, 20 Mar 2025, “FDA Intends to Extend Compliance Date for Food Traceability Rule” — https://www.fda.gov/food/hfp-constituent-updates/fda-intends-extend-compliance-date-food-traceability-rule  \n[7] Lee’s Market internal records: food-safety-compliance.pdf; food-safety-regulatory-compliance.pdf  \n[8] Email from David Lee <david.lee@leesmarket.com> to Emily Patel, 15 Jul 2025, subject “Re: FSMA 204 Compliance Update - Q2 2025”  \n[9] Email from Amy Chen <amy.chen@leesmarket.com> to Emily Patel and Jason Kim, 5 Apr 2024, subject “Q1 2024 Training Update”",
